<section xml:id="notes-week-13">
    <title>Weeks 13 and 14</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading covers chapters 5, 6, and 7</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 17 covers 5.1 through 5.7</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 18 covers 7.1 through 7.5</alert>
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-chap5">
        <title>Spacetime Physics Chapter 5</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Chapter 5 introduces <term>spacetime diagrams</term>, a visualization
                        tool for understanding how events are related in special relativity.
                        This chapter doesn't use the Lorentz transformations, so it focuses
                        on understanding things using the interval <m>x^2-t^2</m>. The idea
                        is that different reference frames with a common origin will see
                        the same event at different points of the invariant hyperbolae.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        In class, we'll discuss how to set up x-t and x<prime/>-t<prime/> 
                        axes corresponding to different inertial frames in the same graph,
                        which will make the geometric picture a bit richer. Sections 5.2
                        through 5.4 walk you through visualizing events with a spacetime diagram.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 5.5 through 5.8 use spacetime diagrams to introduce a geometric
                        analogy for the <term>wristwatch time</term> defined in chapter 1 as the
                        equivalent to the arclength of a curve. With this understanding in hand,
                        we can begin to understand the <q>twin paradox</q>, one of the most famous
                        experiments in special relativity. And I do mean experiment: we haven't been
                        able to send twins to deep space on a starship, but flying atomic clocks
                        around Earth verified this effect remarkably well.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
     <subsection xml:id="subsec-chap6">
        <title>Spacetime Physics Chapter 6</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Chapter 6 is a brief chapter on causality. It discusses the
                        three types of intervals between pairs of events: timelike,
                        spacelike, and lightlike. Timelike separated events are the
                        ones that can influence each other, so the <term>light cone</term>
                        is the new structure that defines <q>past</q> and <q>future</q>
                        in special relativity.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Unlike in newtonian physics, where any event can influence any
                        other event, spacelike separated events are a sort of <q>elsewhere</q>,
                        only becoming accessible to an observer once the light-cone of that event
                        crosses our own.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-chap7">
        <title>Spacetime Physics Chapter 7</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        This chapter introduces concept of <term>4-momentum</term> (note:
                        no one call it <q>momenergy</q>). It gets us started on the road
                        to <m>E=mc^2</m>, and the unification of momentum and energy
                        is a very beautiful consequence of special relativity.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 7.2 is a beautiful geometric argument of what the
                        equivalent of momentum should be in relativity. The geometric
                        picture is new, so we will spend some time in class discussing
                        thought experiments that give further support to this definition.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The rest of the chapter then provides interpretations for the components
                        of the 4-momentum vector, as well as that of the magnitude
                        <m>E^2-p^2</m> or, in conventional units, <m>E^2-p^2c^2</m>. Note that just
                        like the interval, the <q>magnitude</q> squared of a 4 vector in relativity 
                        has a minus sign in its definition.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>